# Claude Code agent worktrees (harness scratch; the branches they hold are the
# real artifact). Only worktrees/ is ignored, so a future .claude/settings.json
# or .claude/agents/ remains trackable.
.claude/worktrees/

# Benchmark corpus artifacts. Only the sources and the per-target `cflags` are
# tracked -- those are the reproducible inputs; everything else is produced by
# build_all.sh or by a run. `flag.txt` holds the target's secret flag, so it
# must never enter history; it was previously untracked only by omission, which
# is not a guarantee. Build artifacts have no extension, hence the explicit
# per-target globs rather than a blanket rule that would also swallow the .c.
benchmark/corpus*/*/flag.txt
benchmark/corpus*/*/payload.bin
benchmark/corpus_vectors/*/ingress_*
!benchmark/corpus_vectors/*/ingress_*.c

# Harness controls (benchmark/controls/) follow the same rule as the corpus: the
# source and its build script are the reproducible inputs, the ELF is produced by
# build.sh. `flag.txt` here holds a planted secret during a validation run and must
# never enter history -- the whole point of these controls is that the secret
# POSTDATES the run, so a committed one would be worthless as well as unsafe.
benchmark/controls/*/flag.txt
benchmark/controls/*/flagfile_win
!benchmark/controls/*/*.c
!benchmark/controls/*/build.sh

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Never-commit artifacts. These were all untracked-but-unignored, which meant a
# single `git add -A` would have committed them; the first entry would have
# committed SECRETS.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# The HTB challenge tree. Each challenge directory ships its real flag, and a flag
# readable from the repo would let a component "solve" a target by reading the
# answer instead of exploiting it -- so this is a measurement-integrity rule as
# well as a secret-handling one. Binaries here are third-party and not ours to
# redistribute either.
tests/htb-targets/

# Generated exploit artifacts from `supwngo solve` / `autopwn` / `report` runs.
# Reproducible from the corpus plus the harness, per-run, and frequently large.
solve_output/
report_output/
exploit.bin

# Verification receipts, written BESIDE the target a run verified. Named
# `.supwngo_receipt_<binary>_<hash>_<offset>_<technique>`, so they accumulate one
# per distinct verified attempt. Ignored globally rather than per-directory
# because the target decides where they land: a run against anything inside a
# TRACKED tree (`tests/fixtures/...`, which is where this rule was earned) leaves
# them committable, and unlike the corpus artifacts there is no build step whose
# absence would hint that they are generated.
.supwngo_receipt_*
